    "science": "Key Active Ingredients: \nCannabis Sativa Seed Oil\n\n Has high content of antioxidants and unsaturated fatty acids that are desirable in formulations because they prevent moisture loss and reduce the occurrence of dry skin.\nhas strong moisturizing properties\nInhibition of enzymes involved in skin aging, inflammation and hyperpigmentation\nHas anti-collagenase, anti-elastase, anti-tyrosinase, and anti-inflammatory activities,\nCannabis oil, especially its primary bioactive constituents, CBD and THC, demonstrates considerable potential in facilitating skin wound healing by modifying oxidative stress via the regulation of reactive oxygen species. CBD’s therapeutic effects in wound healing are largely attributed to its antioxidant, anti-inflammatory, and antimicrobial properties.\nHas antibacterial properties \n\nRef: \nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/350746177_The_beneficial_effect_of_Cannabis_Sativa_seed_oil_on_the_epidermis\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2468227626001572\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2468227626001572#sec0021\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11510192/#sec7-pharmaceutics-16-01277\n\n\nGlycyrrhiza Glabra (Licorice) Root Extract\nThe primary bioactive compounds driving these actions are glabridin, liquiritin, isoliquiritigenin, and glycyrrhizin (glycyrrhizic acid)\nGlycyrrhizin acts similarly to mild topical hydrocortisone. It inhibits the enzyme 11-\\(\\beta \\)-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, prolonging the local anti-inflammatory effects of cortisol natively found in the skin\nglabridin suppresses tyrosinase activity.\nAnti-inflammatory \nSkin Depigmentation effect \nimprove skin barrier function\nantioxidant\nantibacterial, \nanti-aging\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/9/1/7\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378874125006336\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12562305/#sec3-gels-11-00834\nhttps://rjwave.org/jaafr/papers/JAAFR2602605.pdf"
