    "science": "Key Active Ingredients: \nArnica Extract (Arnica Montana)\nHas powerful anti-inflammatory effect\nHas anti-inflammatory activity along the NF-κB—eicosanoid inflammation pathway\nrepresent a valid alternative to non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs\nRef:\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23947690/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10053944/\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25171757/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568507/\n\nMentha Piperita (Peppermint) Oil\n\nSensory Analgesic & Anti-Pruritic (Anti-Itch) Action\nMenthol acts as a selective agonist for the Transient Receptor Potential Melastatin 8 (TRPM8) ion channel, the primary nerve receptor responsible for sensing cold. When applied topically, it triggers a cooling sensation that chemically overrides and blocks pain or itch signals traveling along sensory C-fibers to the brain, providing rapid relief from localized irritation.\nMicrocirculation Boost\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3778045/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12860549/#cbdv70936-sec-0190\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/285996501_Chemical_composition_and_antimicrobial_activity_of_peppermint_Mentha_piperita_L_Essential_oil\nhttp://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6751511/#S0005\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10649426/\nShea Butter (Butyrospermum Parkii Butter)\n\nShowed the presence  of  carbohydrates,  steroids  and  triterpens.  In addition to  a  good DPPH scavenging  activity in qualitative level \nShea butter increased the photostability and in vivo SPF of a molded lipstick sunscreen\nThe main fatty acids found in shea are stearic, oleic, palmitic, linoleic, and arachidic acid, which provide moisturizing and barrier protective actions\nThe unsaponifiables include antioxidants (oil soluble tocopherols), triterpenes (e.g., butyrospermol), phenols, sterols, karitene, allantoin, and polyphenols (mainly the catechin), which together have been shown to provide UV-B absorbing properties\nShea butter has shown to boost collagen production while inactivating proteases such as metalloprotease (e.g., collagenase) as well as serine protease (e.g., elastase)\nHas Anti-inflammatory activity\nIncrease in epidermal thickness\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/341397731_Physical_Properties_Antibacterial_and_Antioxidant_Properties_of_Raw_South_Africa_Shea_Butter_against_Samples_from_Libyan_Market\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jocd.13429\nhttps://d1wqtxts1xzle7.cloudfront.net/80674587/AAK-Shea_butter_extract_for_bioactive_skin_care-2015-libre.pdf?1644701206=&response-content-disposition=inline%3B+filename%3DTriterpene_Esters_Reported_in_the_Litera.pdf&Expires=1780535502&Signature=Q7LOkI3c9j~tA9FZ03iuFlEHdMWxfGOqWniRd2hyKWiKpV~22YMxhbzJRZzYsryYxy7GQx~8gwRnXV1x7jk2v1B3h80J8SANGkdCHKslCECGTRjQBJeOqXYs3S2zzqophBD95bauhJdp8L-g~6tx27Ewg3f5cAB9FhanFIPn2neB0VUaF~aUJQyR~9tqZlwf6HIT--isUeIe0Kg10TA36lz0u2LcGbZpoYG7AdFkgRH3tcJmU7nouWKiCrGrA5V51c~Xhb0Ys4QoNKF2QmGMPpdj~ikCA6FZIliqSnQhECSIsclQ2GXGPUgK4wfMjWf4BHc7S2ihsTPj9ydEXByPZA__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAJLOHF5GGSLRBV4ZA\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8230945/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5933397/\n\n\n\nCastor Seed Oil (Ricinus Communis Seed Oil)\n\nReducing hyperpigmentation \nImproves hydration and elasticity, \nImproves signs of aging through its antioxidant properties\nanti-inflammatory, antimicrobial, and antioxidative properties \nSkin relies entirely on its dominant fatty acid, ricinoleic acid\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12978418/\n\n\n\n\nSunflower Seed Oil (Helianthus Annuus Seed Oil)\n\nThe main chemical components of SFO were oleic and octadecatrienoic acids, which have potential biological activities in wound healing by regulating lipid metabolism and being anti-inflammatory.\nIt combines a high concentration of linoleic acid with natural (alpha)-tocopherol (Vitamin E). This combination allows it to actively speed up barrier recovery and lower transepidermal water loss (TEWL). \n\nRef:\nhttps://farmaciajournal.com/wp-content/uploads/art-15-Maliza_Syaidah_Agusta_1001-1012.pdf\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/390795506_Formulation_of_Serum_Sunflower_Helianthus_annuus_L_Seed_Oil_as_Antiacne_Preparation\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7500752/\n\n\nWaxes (Carnauba Wax, Candelilla Wax, Beeswax, Ozokerite)\nNatural waxes act as hydrophobic, semi-occlusive film-formers. They mimic the skin's natural surface lipids, sealing the fragile, non-keratinized lip tissue to suppress transepidermal water loss (TEWL) and protect against environmental irritants.\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/384299184_Euphorbia_Cerifera_and_Candelilla_Wax_Natural_Emollients_with_Moisturizing_Effects_and_Skin_Barrier_Protection_in_Skincare_Products\n\n\nVitamin E \nhave antioxidant and moisturizing properties \nProtect against the damage of UVB radiation, with emphasis on the reduction of acute erythema and photoaging\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/352470124_Vitamin_E_in_Human_Skin_Functionality_and_Topical_Products\n\n\nWitch Hazel Extract (Hamamelis Virginiana)\nPossess strong antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties.\nantibacterial, \nantifungal, \nantiviral, \nwound healing properties: Hamamelis also promotes wound healing by modulating enzymes, reducing oxidative damage, and stimulating skin cell growth\nClinical studies support its use in eczema, acne, UV-induced erythema, and sensitive scalp conditions\nwith improved skin barrier function\n\n\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12250947/#sec5-molecules-30-02744\nhttps://www.jaad.org/article/S0190-9622(24)02437-X/fulltext\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/386047423_Revealing_the_Therapeutic_Potential_Investigating_the_Impact_of_a_Novel_Witch_Hazel_Formula_on_Anti-Inflammation_and_Antioxidation\n\nAntioxidant Complex (Ascorbic Acid, Ascorbyl Palmitate, Retinyl Palmitate)\nVitamin C is one of the most powerful antioxidants in the skinDue to its antioxidant, neocollagenesis, and skin-lightening properties, its clinical applications range from photoprotection and antiaging to antipigmentation\nRetinyl Palmitate mitigate UVB-induced skin ageing by promoting cell migration, reducing oxidative stress and inflammation through the PPAR-α pathway, and enhancing biocompatibility.\nAscorbyl palmitate operates in a potent cyclic synergy with Vitamin E (\\(\\alpha \\)-tocopherol). When \\(\\alpha \\)-tocopherol neutralizes a lipid free radical, it becomes oxidized and inactive. Ascorbyl palmitate donates an electron to the oxidized tocopheryl radical, converting it back into active Vitamin E to establish a continuous, multi-phase antioxidant shield\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5605218/#sec4\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5579659/#sec3-nutrients-09-00866\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/12/2/68\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5248941/"
