    "science": "Key Active Ingredients: \nPanthenol (10%)\n\nPotent photoprotective and antioxidative properties in mitigating UV radiation-induced skin damage. Effectively reduced oxidative stress, inhibited DNA damage, and prevented apoptosis in human skin organ cultures exposed to UV radiation\n\nMOA: panthenol penetrates the stratum corneum and is converted into pantothenic acid (vitamin B5), an essential component of coenzyme A. This coenzyme plays a pivotal role in various biochemical pathways, including those involved in lipid metabolism and energy production. At the cellular level, pantothenic acid contributes to the synthesis and maintenance of fatty acids and sphingolipids, which are crucial for the integrity and function of the skin barrier.​\n\nA panthenol‐enriched Mask effectively decreased post non‐ablative laser erythema, enhanced skin hydration, and promoted skin barrier repair.\nHygroscopic Humectant: Structurally features a high count of hydroxyl (-OH) groups, allowing it to rapidly attract, bind, and lock water molecules into the outermost skin cells during the washing process\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12232273/\nhttps://cosmeticscience.net/panthenol/\nhttps://trichopartner.pl/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/skin-moisturizing-effects-of-panthenol-based-formulations.pdf\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/8/2/44\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12232337/\n\nShea Butter (Butyrospermum Parkii Butter)\n\nShowed the presence  of  carbohydrates,  steroids  and  triterpens.  In addition to  a  good DPPH scavenging  activity in qualitative level \nShea butter increased the photostability and in vivo SPF of a molded sunscreen\nskin-conditioning agents\nThe main fatty acids found in shea are stearic, oleic, palmitic, linoleic, and arachidic acid, which provide moisturizing and barrier protective actions\nThe unsaponifiables include antioxidants (oil soluble tocopherols), triterpenes (e.g., butyrospermol), phenols, sterols, karitene, allantoin, and polyphenols (mainly the catechin), which together have been shown to provide UV-B absorbing properties\nShea butter has shown to boost collagen production while inactivating proteases such as metalloprotease (e.g., collagenase) as well as serine protease (e.g., elastase)\nHas Anti-inflammatory activity\nIncrease in epidermal thickness\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/341397731_Physical_Properties_Antibacterial_and_Antioxidant_Properties_of_Raw_South_Africa_Shea_Butter_against_Samples_from_Libyan_Market\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jocd.13429\nhttps://d1wqtxts1xzle7.cloudfront.net/80674587/AAK-Shea_butter_extract_for_bioactive_skin_care-2015-libre.pdf?1644701206=&response-content-disposition=inline%3B+filename%3DTriterpene_Esters_Reported_in_the_Litera.pdf&Expires=1780535502&Signature=Q7LOkI3c9j~tA9FZ03iuFlEHdMWxfGOqWniRd2hyKWiKpV~22YMxhbzJRZzYsryYxy7GQx~8gwRnXV1x7jk2v1B3h80J8SANGkdCHKslCECGTRjQBJeOqXYs3S2zzqophBD95bauhJdp8L-g~6tx27Ewg3f5cAB9FhanFIPn2neB0VUaF~aUJQyR~9tqZlwf6HIT--isUeIe0Kg10TA36lz0u2LcGbZpoYG7AdFkgRH3tcJmU7nouWKiCrGrA5V51c~Xhb0Ys4QoNKF2QmGMPpdj~ikCA6FZIliqSnQhECSIsclQ2GXGPUgK4wfMjWf4BHc7S2ihsTPj9ydEXByPZA__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAJLOHF5GGSLRBV4ZA\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8230945/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5933397/\n\n\n\n\nAloe Barbadensis Leaf Juice\n\npossesses anti-inflammatory effects that soothe irritated skin and reduce redness\nThe antimicrobial activity of aloe vera is attributed to its rich composition of bioactive compounds, including polysaccharides, vitamins, and phenolic compounds, which work synergistically to combat acne-causing bacteria, such as Propionibacterium acnes\nAcceleration of topical wound healing\nSkin Brightening/Antioxidant\nAloin, polysaccharides, vitamins, Acemannan on Aloe Reduces hyperpigmentation, retains skin moisture, heals sun burns, promotes collagen and elastin production, wound healing\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/384300038_Aloe_Barbadensis_Leaf_Juice_Unveiling_Aloe_Vera's_Antimicrobial_Qualities_for_Acne_Treatment_and_Prevention_in_Natural_Skincare\nhttps://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13369-022-07283-6#Sec27\nhttps://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK92765/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11899789/\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0254629923002971#bib0034\n\n\nArnica Extract\nHas powerful anti-inflammatory effect\nHas anti-inflammatory activity along the NF-κB—eicosanoid inflammation pathway\nrepresent a valid alternative to non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs\nRef:\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23947690/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10053944/\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25171757/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568507/\n\nLinum Usitatissimum (Flaxseed) Seed Oil\nmajor components present in L. usitatissimum oil extract are linolic, palmitic and oleic acid\nMOA : The high concentration of \\(\\alpha \\)-linolenic acid (ALA, an omega-3 fatty acid) decreases the synthesis of inflammatory mediators such as \\(PGE_{2}\\) (prostaglandin E2) and leukotrienes\n\nLinoleic acid (\\(\\omega \\)-6) is a precursor to membrane phospholipids that provide skin integrity and fluidity, making it highly effective in anti-aging and moisturizing formulations\nHas anti-inflammatory,\n Antimicrobial, \nAntioxidant activities. \nalso stimulates fibroblast proliferation in the skin tissue\nRef:\nhttps://www.traumamon.com/article_100118_754919ca60dc0e41d56684ef3a751bf1.pdf\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/353239568_Flaxseed_Linum_usitatissimum_Extract_Activity_on_Human_Oral_Fibroblasts_HOrF_Cell_Line\n\nGlycyrrhiza Glabra (Licorice) Root Extract\nThe primary bioactive compounds driving these actions are glabridin, liquiritin, isoliquiritigenin, and glycyrrhizin (glycyrrhizic acid)\nGlycyrrhizin acts similarly to mild topical hydrocortisone. It inhibits the enzyme 11-\\(\\beta \\)-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, prolonging the local anti-inflammatory effects of cortisol natively found in the skin\nglabridin suppresses tyrosinase activity.\nAnti-inflammatory \nSkin Depigmentation effect \nimprove skin barrier function\nantioxidant\nantibacterial, \nanti-aging\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/9/1/7\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378874125006336\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12562305/#sec3-gels-11-00834\nhttps://rjwave.org/jaafr/papers/JAAFR2602605.pdf"
