    "science": "Key Active Ingredients: \nHyaluronic Acid \nMOA: Topical hyaluronic acid (HA) acts primarily as a high-capacity humectant, binding up to 1,000 times its weight in water to the skin surface to provide immediate hydration, plumping, and smoothing effects. It works by forming a breathable, moisture-retaining film, decreasing transepidermal water loss (TEWL) while reducing fine lines and enhancing skin elasticity.\n•\tEnhances hydration \n•\tSupports collagen function and skin structure\n\nTopical hyaluronic acid (HA) is scientifically proven to significantly increase skin hydration (up to 134% immediately, ~55% at 6 weeks) and improve elasticity. \nRef :\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10078143/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8322246/#:~:text=The%20HA%20serum%20demonstrated%20excellent,an%20excellent%20skin%20tolerability%20profile.\n\nShea Butter (Butyrospermum Parkii Butter) 3%\n\nShowed the presence  of  carbohydrates,  steroids  and  triterpens.  In addition to a  good DPPH scavenging  activity in qualitative level \nShea butter increased the photostability and in vivo SPF of a molded lipstick sunscreen\nskin-conditioning agents\nThe main fatty acids found in shea are stearic, oleic, palmitic, linoleic, and arachidic acid, which provide moisturizing and barrier protective actions\nThe unsaponifiables include antioxidants (oil soluble tocopherols), triterpenes (e.g., butyrospermol), phenols, sterols, karitene, allantoin, and polyphenols (mainly the catechin), which together have been shown to provide UV-B absorbing properties\nShea butter has shown to boost collagen production while inactivating proteases such as metalloprotease (e.g., collagenase) as well as serine protease (e.g., elastase)\nHas Anti-inflammatory activity\nIncrease in epidermal thickness\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/341397731_Physical_Properties_Antibacterial_and_Antioxidant_Properties_of_Raw_South_Africa_Shea_Butter_against_Samples_from_Libyan_Market\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jocd.13429\nhttps://d1wqtxts1xzle7.cloudfront.net/80674587/AAK-Shea_butter_extract_for_bioactive_skin_care-2015-libre.pdf?1644701206=&response-content-disposition=inline%3B+filename%3DTriterpene_Esters_Reported_in_the_Litera.pdf&Expires=1780535502&Signature=Q7LOkI3c9j~tA9FZ03iuFlEHdMWxfGOqWniRd2hyKWiKpV~22YMxhbzJRZzYsryYxy7GQx~8gwRnXV1x7jk2v1B3h80J8SANGkdCHKslCECGTRjQBJeOqXYs3S2zzqophBD95bauhJdp8L-g~6tx27Ewg3f5cAB9FhanFIPn2neB0VUaF~aUJQyR~9tqZlwf6HIT--isUeIe0Kg10TA36lz0u2LcGbZpoYG7AdFkgRH3tcJmU7nouWKiCrGrA5V51c~Xhb0Ys4QoNKF2QmGMPpdj~ikCA6FZIliqSnQhECSIsclQ2GXGPUgK4wfMjWf4BHc7S2ihsTPj9ydEXByPZA__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAJLOHF5GGSLRBV4ZA\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8230945/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5933397/\n\n\n\nAvocado Oil (Persea Gratissima Oil)\n\nAntioxidant properties by scavenging in vitro free radicals, DPPH, and H2O2 and protecting against UVB-damaged fibroblasts by reducing intracellular ROS in UVB-irradiated cells.\nThe benefits of avocado oil in skincare stem from its nutrient composition:\nOleic Acid (Omega-9): Helps improve skin softness and supports hydration.\nLinoleic Acid (Omega-6): Linked with ceramide production, supporting barrier function.\nPalmitic Acid: Contributes to a protective skin feel.\nVitamin E (Tocopherols): Known antioxidant that helps reduce oxidative stress.\nCarotenoids: Provide antioxidant potential and help protect from environmental stressors.\nSqualene: Soothes and softens skin.\nSterols & Unsaponifiables: Support skin conditioning and comfort.\n\n\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12314098/#Sec6\n\n\n\nGlycyrrhiza Glabra (Licorice) Root Extract\n\nThe primary bioactive compounds driving these actions are glabridin, liquiritin, isoliquiritigenin, and glycyrrhizin (glycyrrhizic acid)\nGlycyrrhizin acts similarly to mild topical hydrocortisone. It inhibits the enzyme 11-\\(\\beta \\)-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, prolonging the local anti-inflammatory effects of cortisol natively found in the skin\nglabridin suppresses tyrosinase activity.\nAnti-inflammatory \nSkin Depigmentation effect \nimprove skin barrier function\nantioxidant\nantibacterial, \nanti-aging\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/9/1/7\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378874125006336\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12562305/#sec3-gels-11-00834\nhttps://rjwave.org/jaafr/papers/JAAFR2602605.pdf\n\nOryza Sativa (Rice) Extract\n\nTyrosinase & Melanogenesis Inhibitor: Suppresses the rate-limiting enzymes responsible for melanin synthesis, resulting in skin-lightening and hyperpigmentation reduction \n Moisturizing\nAnti-aging \nAnti-oxidant \nRef: \nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37840342/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11394965/\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/12/1/29\nhttps://academicjournals.org/journal/JPP/article-full-text-pdf/955B6C073586\n\n\nRetinyl Palmitate (Vitamin A)\n\nRetinyl Palmitate mitigate UVB-induced skin ageing by promoting cell migration, reducing oxidative stress and inflammation through the PPAR-α pathway, and enhancing biocompatibility.\nFirst choice for anti-oxidation and anti-aging, and it is also an effective anti-aging ingredient recommended by many dermatologists in the United States.\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10622759/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10622759/#s4\n\n\n\nTocopheryl Acetate (Vitamin E)\n\nhave antioxidant and moisturizing properties \nProtect against the damage of UVB radiation, with emphasis on the reduction of acute erythema and photoaging\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/352470124_Vitamin_E_in_Human_Skin_Functionality_and_Topical_Products\n\n\nA blend of essential fatty acids: \nLauric Acid, Myristic Acid, Palmitic Acid, Oleic Acid and Arachidic Acid\nFatty acid-based formulations play a crucial role in restoring skin homeostasis.\nFatty Acids as Chemical Penetration Enhancers\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12268455/#sec11\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2898574/#S27"
