    "howToUse": "Massage into hands as often as needed.",
    "science": "Key Active Ingredients: \nAlpha Arbutin \n\nSkin Depigmenting Agent \nAntimelanogenic \nAntioxidant Properties\nMOA: depigmenting mechanism of arbutin in humans involves inhibition of melanosomal tyrosinase activity\nα-arbutin has strong anti-photoaging and whitening abilities\n\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8301119/\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8632348/\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/29/9/1921\n\n\nGlutathione  \n\nServes as a potent antioxidant in human body\nhas a skin-whitening effect in humans through its tyrosinase inhibitory activity,\nit acts as a primary antioxidant, detoxifier, and redox regulator, preserving cellular integrity by interacting with key enzymes, including glutathione peroxidase, glutathione reductase, and glutathione transferase. Its impact extends to keratinocyte proliferation, fibroblast function, melanogenesis, and extracellular matrix remodeling, all of which are crucial for maintaining skin homeostasis, facilitating regeneration, and enhancing resilience against aging.\n\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7196133/#B6\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25378941/\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/31/6/981\n\nCitrus Limon (Lemon) Fruit Extract \n \nantioxidant, anti-inflammatory, antimicrobial, skin-brightening, preservative, and substance-penetrating properties\nInhibits tyrosinase activity and reduces melanin formation, showing promise in cosmetics for controlling skin pigmentation and promoting even-toned, brighter skin.\nantioxidant, whitening, antibacterial, anti-inflammatory, and wound-healing activities.\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/382741103_Cosmetological_applications_of_Citrus_limon_A_mini-review\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11985412/\n\n\nShea Butter (Butyrospermum Parkii Butter)\n\nShowed the  presence  of  carbohydrates,  steroids  and  triterpens.  In addition to  a  good DPPH scavenging  activity in qualitative level \nShea butter increased the photostability and in vivo SPF of a molded lipstick sunscreen\nskin-conditioning agents\nThe main fatty acids found in shea are stearic, oleic, palmitic, linoleic, and arachidic acid, which provide moisturizing and barrier protective actions\nThe unsaponifiables include antioxidants (oil soluble tocopherols), triterpenes (e.g., butyrospermol), phenols, sterols, karitene, allantoin, and polyphenols (mainly the catechin), which together have been shown to provide UV-B absorbing properties\nShea butter has shown to boost collagen production while inactivating proteases such as metalloprotease (e.g., collagenase) as well as serine protease (e.g., elastase)\nHas Anti-inflammatory activity\nIncrease in epidermal thickness\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/341397731_Physical_Properties_Antibacterial_and_Antioxidant_Properties_of_Raw_South_Africa_Shea_Butter_against_Samples_from_Libyan_Market\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/jocd.13429\nhttps://d1wqtxts1xzle7.cloudfront.net/80674587/AAK-Shea_butter_extract_for_bioactive_skin_care-2015-libre.pdf?1644701206=&response-content-disposition=inline%3B+filename%3DTriterpene_Esters_Reported_in_the_Litera.pdf&Expires=1780535502&Signature=Q7LOkI3c9j~tA9FZ03iuFlEHdMWxfGOqWniRd2hyKWiKpV~22YMxhbzJRZzYsryYxy7GQx~8gwRnXV1x7jk2v1B3h80J8SANGkdCHKslCECGTRjQBJeOqXYs3S2zzqophBD95bauhJdp8L-g~6tx27Ewg3f5cAB9FhanFIPn2neB0VUaF~aUJQyR~9tqZlwf6HIT--isUeIe0Kg10TA36lz0u2LcGbZpoYG7AdFkgRH3tcJmU7nouWKiCrGrA5V51c~Xhb0Ys4QoNKF2QmGMPpdj~ikCA6FZIliqSnQhECSIsclQ2GXGPUgK4wfMjWf4BHc7S2ihsTPj9ydEXByPZA__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAJLOHF5GGSLRBV4ZA\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8230945/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5933397/\n\n\nPrunus Amygdalus Dulcis (Sweet Almond) Oil\n\n\nPrunus dulcis (almond) oil is widely recognized for its emollient, moisturizing, and skin-nourishing properties. \nStrengthen stratum corneum integrity, recovery and lipid ratio.\nRef:\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/392493521_Formulation_and_Evaluation_Prunus_Dulcis_Almond_Oil_Moisturizing_Cream\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34957578/\n\n\n\nCocos Nucifera (Coconut) Oil \n \nanti inflammatory activity\nby suppressing inflammatory markers and protecting the skin by enhancing skin barrier function.\nAnti-inflammatory and antioxidant property exerts anti-inflammatory activity by inhibiting various cytokines, including TNF-α, IFNγ, IL-6, IL-5, and IL-8, Downregulated (inducible nitric oxide (NO) synthase) iNOS expression,  Decreased cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) expressions, Inhibited tumour necrosis factors (TNF-α) activity. Downregulated Interleukin-6 (IL-6) expression,  Enhanced Glutathione (GSH) and superoxide dismutase (SOD) activity, ecreased lipid peroxidation, increased expression of collagen and hyaluronan synthase-3\nMoisturiser\nPhotoprotection\nWound healing\nRef:\n\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6335493/#abs0010\nhttps://ijdvl.com/coconut-oil-natures-own-emollient/\n\n\nSqualane \n\nSqualene appears to be critical for reducing free radical oxidative damage to the skin.\nEmollient\nrestoring healthy suppleness and flexibility without leaving an oily residue\nAntioxidation\nsqualene is a highly effective oxygen-scavenging agent.\nProtecting UV-Induced Inhibition of Collagen Biosynthesis and Wound Healing in Human Dermal Fibroblast\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6253993/\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/30/9/1964\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nUV filters \n\n\nINCI Name |  | UV Spectrum Coverage\nEthylhexyl Methoxycinnamate |  | UVB\nEthylhexyl Salicylate |  | UVB\nDiethylamino Hydroxybenzoyl Hexyl Benzoate |  | UVA1\nMethylene Bis-Benzotriazolyl Tetramethylbutylphenol (nano) |  | Broad-spectrum UVA + UVB\n\nProvides protection against UVA/UVB rays, helping prevent sun-induced pigmentation and premature aging of the hands.\nRef:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/10/4/101\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8361399/"
