    "howToUse": "Apply a small amount of the Wash Gel on damp hand, wash gently intimate area and rinse thoroughly.",
    "science": "Key Active Ingredients: \nAlpha Arbutin \n\nSkin Depigmenting Agent \nAntimelanogenic \nAntioxidant Properties\nMOA: depigmenting mechanism of arbutin in humans involves inhibition of melanosomal tyrosinase activity\nα-arbutin has strong anti-photoaging and whitening abilities\n\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8301119/\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8632348/\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/1420-3049/29/9/1921\n\nNiacinamide \n\nEffective for management of Hyper-Pigmentation \nMOA: inhibits the transfer of melanosomes from melanocytes to the surrounding skin cells (keratinocytes) by 35% to 68% \n\nNiacinamide acts on a cellular level to protect the extracellular matrix and reverse apparent signs of aging\nMOA: It acts as a precursor to the endogenous antioxidants NADH and NADPH, which fuel DNA repair, boost collagen production, and block the spontaneous oxidative cross-linking (glycation) that yellows aging skin \nClinical research shows that niacinamide deeply restores compromised or dry skin\nMOA: It upregulates the synthesis of epidermal ceramides, free fatty acids, and cholesterol in keratinocytes, preventing transepidermal water loss (TEWL)\n\nIt exhibits sebostatic and anti-inflammatory properties that calm the swelling and redness \n\nRef:\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12100180/\nhttps://cosmoderma.org/niacinamide-efficacy-in-skin-therapy-the-multitasking-marvel-for-glowing-skin-a-comprehensive-literature-review/\nhttps://www.jaad.org/article/S0190-9622(04)02915-9/fulltext\nhttps://cosmoderma.org/niacinamide-efficacy-in-skin-therapy-the-multitasking-marvel-for-glowing-skin-a-comprehensive-literature-review/\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-4362.1995.tb04449.x\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32983333/\n\n\nPaeonia Albiflora Root Extract \n\nReduce melanin content, with a trend toward decreased AGE(advanced glycation end products) accumulation and visible spots on the cheeks\nhelp reduce skin dullness\ncontaining paeoniflorin in cosmetic or dermatological applications for reducing the severity of some hyperpigmented skin disorders.\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/12/4/163\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26826350/\n\n\nLactic Acid\n\nExternal feminine washes, particularly those containing lactic acid and formulated to an acidic pH that enhances skin homeostasis, are considered more appropriate than internal washes or douches and may be a useful adjunct therapy for women with vaginal infections or taking antibiotics\nSuitable for various skin types, including dry and sensitive skin.\nhas antibacterial effects, immune regulation, and promotion of wound healing\nIt was shown to decrease melanin synthesis by directly inhibiting the tyrosinase enzyme, thereby explaining its skin whitening effects\nRef:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-6382/9/2/55\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11055559/#s0005\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/380003968_Lactic_Acid_Chemical_Peeling_in_Skin_Disorders\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6053170/\n\nChamomile Extract \n\nstrong antibacterial and anti-inflammatory activity \nChamomile oil targets the root of chronic itching and swelling. On a molecular level, \\(\\alpha \\)-bisabolol and chamazulene downregulate Th17 cell\nAntioxidant, anti-inflammatory, analgesic\nHas anti-inflammatory agent aseffective as steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs andnon-steroidal currently marketed.\nRef:\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30414245/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9611340/#sec5-pharmaceuticals-15-01284\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/289263589_Topical_effects_of_Chamomilla_Recutita_in_skin_damage_A_literature_review\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/chemistry/chamazulene\n\nAllantoin\nAllantoin functions as a multi-stage cell-regenerating agent. It speeds up the skin's transition from an inflammatory state to a proliferative phase, making it a staple for wound healing, scar reduction, and severe barrier repair\nHas anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects. It has been shown to repair cutaneous photodamage and reduce inflammation following UVR exposure. \nMOA: It operates by strictly regulating the localized inflammatory response, directly stimulating fibroblastic proliferation, and accelerating the synthesis of the extracellular matrix (dermal scaffolding)\nRef:\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20877959/\nhttps://www.jaad.org/article/S0190-9622(17)30663-1/abstract\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/46580052_Profile_of_wound_healing_process_induced_by_allantoin\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/allantoin\n\nSodium Hyaluronate \nMOA: Topical hyaluronic acid (HA) acts primarily as a high-capacity humectant, binding up to 1,000 times its weight in water to the skin surface to provide immediate hydration, plumping, and smoothing effects. It works by forming a breathable, moisture-retaining film, decreasing transepidermal water loss (TEWL) while reducing fine lines and enhancing skin elasticity.\n•\tEnhances hydration \n•\tSupports collagen function and skin structure\nTopical hyaluronic acid (HA) is scientifically proven to significantly increase skin hydration (up to 134% immediately, ~55% at 6 weeks) and improve elasticity. \nRef :\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10078143/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8322246/#:~:text=The%20HA%20serum%20demonstrated%20excellent,an%20excellent%20skin%20tolerability%20profile.\n\nSodium PCA \nNatural moisturizing factor (NMF) component for hydration \nImprove the condition of dry skin\n\nRef:\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19469932/\n\n\nInositol, Glycine, Fructose, Urea \nkey components of the skin's Natural Moisturizing Factor (NMF)\nInositol improve skin elasticity and the amount of sebum for dry and oily skin types adjusted to the amount of sebum of the neutral skin types.\nGlycine is believed to help support the production and stimulation of healthy collagen and to be able to penetrate the skin as one of the smallest molecular amino acids. In skin care, glycine is mainly used to improve moisturizing ability, increase collagen production, and promote skin repair and regeneration. These properties make it a valuable ingredient in anti-aging skin care products\nUrea plays a fundamental role in regulating keratinocyte proliferation, the skin's barrier function and antimicrobial defense and maintain healthy skin\n\nRef:\nhttps://scholar.kyobobook.co.kr/article/detail/4040045519279\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11011925/\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8611129/"
