    "science": "Key Active Ingredients: \nSalicylic Acid (BHA) 1%\nStrong efficacy in reducing sebum levels, improving skin hydration, enhancing skin barrier function, and reducing acne severity over 21 days\nkeratolytic action, anti-inflammatory capabilities, and antibacterial activity.\nlowering acne lesions, lessening post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, and smoothing out skin.\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12274963/\nhttps://www.biotech-asia.org/vol22no4/emerging-formulations-and-clinical-applications-of-topical-salicylic-acid-in-acne-management/\n\nMandelic Acid (AHA) 1%\nMandelic acid, a lipophilic alpha hydroxy acid, reduces lipid production, enhances exfoliation and provides clinical and patient perceivable benefits to oily and photodamaged skin\nMandelic acid is defined as a peeling agent that penetrates the skin gradually and uniformly, making it suitable for sensitive skin. It has anti-inflammatory effects and is often used in combination peels for conditions such as melasma, with minimal side effects and quick reepithelization.\n\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.jaad.org/article/S0190-9622(20)31574-7/abstract\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/282774826_Evaluation_of_the_efficacy_and_tolerability_of_mandelic_acid-containing_cosmetic_formulations_for_acne_skin_care\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/mandelic-acid\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30513536/\n \nAzelaic Acid 1%\nantibacterial, anti-inflammatory and reducing excessive keratinization.\nits anti-infective and anti-inflammatory effects. AZA is a competitive inhibitor of mitochondrial oxidoreductases and 5α-reductase, inhibiting the conversion of testosterone to 5-dihydrotestosterone\nAzelaic acid (AzA) is defined as a topical agent effective in the treatment of papulopustular rosacea, demonstrating significant reductions in inflammatory lesions and erythema severity compared to placebo and being equally effective as metronidazole in clinical studies.\nantibacterial, keratolytic, depigmenting, antioxidant, and anti-inflammatory effects.\nAZA is FDA-approved for treating PPR, and research supports its use in melasma and as a second-line treatment for acne vulgaris.\n\nMOA: The molecular mechanism of action of AZA is broad, one of which is thioredoxin inhibition. The thioredoxin enzyme regulates tyrosinase activity through a feedback mechanism involving electron transfer to intracellular thioredoxin, followed by a specific interaction between reduced thioredoxin and tyrosinase [14, 15]. In addition, the thioredoxin/thioredoxin reductase system has been shown to be a major electron donor for DNA-regulating ribonucleotide reductases [16]. The inhibition of thioredoxin reductase by AZA provides a rationale for both its depigmenting properties and the reversible inhibition of DNA synthesis observed in cultured epidermal cells, as well as in some bacteria associated with acne vulgaris.\n\nRef:\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10809820/\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/azelaic-acid\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11512533/\n\nNiacinamide \n\nEffective for management of Hyper-Pigmentation \nMOA: inhibits the transfer of melanosomes from melanocytes to the surrounding skin cells (keratinocytes) by 35% to 68% \n\nNiacinamide acts on a cellular level to protect the extracellular matrix and reverse apparent signs of aging\nMOA: It acts as a precursor to the endogenous antioxidants NADH and NADPH, which fuel DNA repair, boost collagen production, and block the spontaneous oxidative cross-linking (glycation) that yellows aging skin \nClinical research shows that niacinamide deeply restores compromised or dry skin\nMOA: It upregulates the synthesis of epidermal ceramides, free fatty acids, and cholesterol in keratinocytes, preventing transepidermal water loss (TEWL)\n\nIt exhibits sebostatic and anti-inflammatory properties that calm the swelling and redness caused by inflammatory acne.\n\n\n**4% nicotinamide gel is of comparable efficacy to 1% clindamycin gel in the treatment of acne vulgaris. Because topical clindamycin, like other antimicrobials, is associated with emergence of resistant microorganisms, nicotinamide gel is a desirable alternative treatment for acne vulgaris.\n\n\nRef:\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12100180/\nhttps://cosmoderma.org/niacinamide-efficacy-in-skin-therapy-the-multitasking-marvel-for-glowing-skin-a-comprehensive-literature-review/\nhttps://www.jaad.org/article/S0190-9622(04)02915-9/fulltext\nhttps://cosmoderma.org/niacinamide-efficacy-in-skin-therapy-the-multitasking-marvel-for-glowing-skin-a-comprehensive-literature-review/\nhttps://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/j.1365-4362.1995.tb04449.x\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32983333/\n\nGlycyrrhiza Glabra (Licorice) Root Extract\n\nThe primary bioactive compounds driving these actions are glabridin, liquiritin, isoliquiritigenin, and glycyrrhizin (glycyrrhizic acid)\nGlycyrrhizin acts similarly to mild topical hydrocortisone. It inhibits the enzyme 11-\\(\\beta \\)-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, prolonging the local anti-inflammatory effects of cortisol natively found in the skin\nglabridin suppresses tyrosinase activity.\nAnti-inflammatory \nSkin Depigmentation effect \nimprove skin barrier function\nantioxidant\nantibacterial, \nanti-aging\n\nRef:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2079-9284/9/1/7\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0378874125006336\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12562305/#sec3-gels-11-00834\nhttps://rjwave.org/jaafr/papers/JAAFR2602605.pdf\n\nSalix Alba (Willow Bark) Extract\n \nGentle Keratolytic / Exfoliant: Promotes the desquamation (shedding) of the stratum corneum. Unlike synthetic salicylic acid, it accelerates cell turnover without disrupting the lipid barrier or causing skin flaking.\nAstringent: High natural tannin content cross-links surface proteins, which temporarily tightens skin tissue, reduces excess sebum output, and minimizes the appearance of pores.\nAntioxidant (Radical Scavenger): Rich in flavonoids (such as quercetin and rutin) and polyphenols that neutralize reactive oxygen species (ROS) induced by UV radiation and pollution\nRef:\n https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/salicylic-acid\nhttps://www.researchgate.net/publication/394543818_Anti-inflammatory_activity_of_willow_bark_extract_Salix_alba_and_its_effect_on_inflammation_markers_in_the_human_body\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7600001/"
